  <main class="page">
    <header>
      <h1>Physical & Field-Assisted Methods</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        A working domain of my experimental R&amp;D profile, focused on physical treatment
        methods, plasma/RF/PEF systems, electric-field-assisted processes, prototype setups,
        and process-oriented laboratory testing.
      </p>

      <nav>
        <a href="index.html">Profile</a>
        <a href="graph.html">Research Graph</a>
        <a href="functional-materials.html">Functional Materials</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Domain Overview</h2>
      <p>
        This domain brings together experimental methods where physical fields, energy input,
        process conditions, and prototype-level setups are used to affect materials, surfaces,
        biological systems, or process behavior.
      </p>

      <p class="tagline">
        The focus is on practical experimental corridors: how a physical method can be shaped,
        tested, adjusted, and connected to a material or biological application.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What This Domain Includes</h2>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Plasma-Related Processes</h3>
          <p>
            Experimental interest in plasma-based, plasma-related treatment methods and plasma-assisted nanomaterial synthesis,
            especially in relation to surfaces, materials, activation, functional modification and metal-oxide nanomateals syntheis.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>RF Systems</h3>
          <p>
            Work connected to radio-frequency systems, matching concepts, field coupling,
            experimental setup development, and process-oriented testing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Pulsed Electric Fields</h3>
          <p>
            Exploration of pulsed electric field concepts, treatment cells, field-assisted
            interactions, and possible applications in microbiological or bio-related systems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>High-Voltage Prototyping</h3>
          <p>
            Prototype-level work with high-voltage and pulsed systems, focusing on experimental
            feasibility, measurement, safety awareness, and controlled laboratory exploration.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Process Testing</h3>
          <p>
            Practical testing of how process parameters, energy delivery, geometry, timing,
            and material conditions influence experimental outcomes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Measurement & Observation</h3>
          <p>
            Attention to observable effects, process traces, temperature, signal behavior,
            stability, repeatability, and the limits of early-stage experimental interpretation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Experimental Direction</h2>
      <p>
        My work in this domain is not limited to one fixed technology. It is centered on how
        physical methods can be used as experimental tools for modifying, activating, treating,
        or testing systems under real laboratory constraints.
      </p>

      <p>
        This includes building and adjusting prototype pathways, identifying what can be tested
        safely, and separating early experimental traces from validated technological claims.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Example: Field-Assisted Experimental Setups</h2>
      <p>
        One recurring direction in this domain is the development and testing of field-assisted
        setups, where electrical, RF, plasma-related, or pulsed processes are connected with
        material or bio-related questions.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Positioning:</strong>
        this domain is presented as an experimental R&amp;D layer, not as a set of finalized
        devices or commercial systems. The value lies in building working corridors for testing,
        observation, and later refinement.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Relation to Other Domains</h2>
      <p>
        Physical and field-assisted methods connect directly with functional materials,
        surface engineering, coatings, nanomaterial systems, and antimicrobial applications.
        They also connect with AI-assisted R&amp;D workflows, where experimental conditions,
        observations, failures, and process traces can be documented and structured.
      </p>

      <p>
        In this sense, plasma, RF, PEF, and related methods are not isolated techniques.
        They are part of a broader research graph involving material response, process control,
        observation, safety, interpretation, and future development.
      </p>
    </section>

